Some interesting stuff from today. So, unsurprisingly, the structure of the engine looks kinda like this:
while (true) {
	unk_funcs();
	// lots of little functions I haven&#039;t gotten to yet
	Engine_DoTick();
	// like one more function I haven&#039;t gotten to yet. 
}
That function is what I spent some time on today (along with random stuff.)
That Engine_DoTick looks like this in Psuedocode. I assume that since I’m writing this myself, it’s a transformative work. Plus, SEGA isn’t full of the sorta buttheads that Nintendo is full of, I hope.
void Engine_DoTick(void) {
// haven&#039;t gotten around to figuring out what this variable comes from, but... eh the name tracks. I think it&#039;s *probably* a way to allow a state to only run one function per tick?
	void* functionToCall;
	if (g_SkipFunctions != 0) {
		g_lastState_maybe = g_currentState;
		g_currentState = g_nextState;
		functionToCall = g_StateTable[g_currentState].func1;
		getStateName();
		functionToCall();
	}
	g_StateTable[g_currentState].func2();
	if (g_SkipFunctions != 0) {
		g_StateTable[g_currentState].func3();
	}
}
Now, that could be conceived of as being almost interesting, especially if you compare it to any of the actually interesting parts of the engine (coming soon to a write-up near you). The slightly more interesting part is that getStateName()  function. It’s crazy simple, just looks something like this:
char* getStateName() {
	return g_StateNamesTable[g_currentState];
}
I think it was leftover from debugging and the useful bits got ifdefed out or something? There are so many leftover debug pieces and other downright weird things in this binary (at some point I’ll need to figure out why it has 4 copies of the entire periodic table in string form just sitting in prod). But because of this, we have a table of what the engineers called each state, which is currently just interesting but will probably become super helpful at some point. Those are
