I’m digging more into the state machine stuff, looking through the variables… It’s sorta odd what it does in a lot of cases: (here are a few pieces of pseudocode)
if ((g_currentState != 0x13) &amp;&amp; (g_currentState == 0x1b) &amp;&amp; ... (some other global check) ) {
	// do stuff here
}
Like that’s perfectly valid c/c++… but it’s just super weird! I would think it’s a quick-failthrough, which I have seen before, but it’s weird!
Also, I finally figured out what this is saying. Somehow I just totally misread the function. Here’s more accurate pseudocode. The first function call is actually based on the “g_nextState” like so.
void Engine_DoTick(void) {
// haven&#039;t gotten around to figuring out what this variable comes from, but... eh the name tracks. I think it&#039;s *probably* a way to allow a state to only run one function per tick?
	void* functionToCall;
	if (g_changeState != 0) {
		g_lastState_maybe = g_currentState;
		g_currentState = g_nextState;
		functionToCall = g_StateTable[g_nextState].func1;
		getStateName();
		functionToCall();
	}
	g_StateTable[g_currentState].func2();
	if (g_changeState != 0) {
		g_StateTable[g_currentState].func3();
	}
}
I realized this since I found a function that sets changeState, and I was wondering why it would set the flag at the same time it updates nextState
void SM_ChangeState (newstate) {
	g_changeState = 1;
	g_nextState = newstate;
}
The cool thing is what this tells us about the StateTable functions. So we now can guess that, at first function is the “enter state” function, setting things up (since it gets executed with “nextState”), the middle function is the “execute state” and the .  the first function is the “exit” function, cleaning things up.
void Engine_DoTick(void) {
// haven&#039;t gotten around to figuring out what this variable comes from, but... eh the name tracks. I think it&#039;s *probably* a way to allow a state to only run one function per tick?
	void* functionToCall;
	if (g_ChangeState != 0) {
		g_lastState_maybe = g_currentState;
		g_currentState = g_nextState;
		functionToCall = g_StateTable[g_NextState].enterState;
		getStateName();
		functionToCall();
	}
	g_StateTable[g_currentState].func2();
	if (g_ChangeState != 0) {
		g_StateTable[g_currentState].func3();
	}
}
That also leads me to this function

void SM_SetupGlobals(void)

{
	g_StateChange = 1;
	g_NextState = 0;
	g_PrevState = 0;
	g_CurrentState = 0;
	// set some other globals I&#039;ll figure out later
  return;
}

It handles starting it up in a clever way. It flips on g_StateChange, and sets both the current and next state to state 0. You may worry that, given our example, it will also run the “exit state” code. Apparently they thought of this, as that “exit state” function for system is a stub. I have noticed, as I just glanced through, that most exit functions are very short.

