I wish I had more updates today, I’m just glad I did some work on it.
First, I went through and had a script label all of the functions used for the init, handle, and exit for states. So thats 108 new functions labeled, which is cool I suppose. I really do need to start actually digging into some of these though.
I did find some cool stuff eventually though, including some new functions. Before I go back to bed. Can I first extoll the virtues of figuring out what your memory functions (alloc, free, etc) are before you get crazy far into a project? They offer such great anchors. That and your string functions.
Anyway, so I was digging through engine code, specifically SM_ENDING_Init. It had a ton of random functions, so I started working on the very last function, which I am now calling ClearAllSlots. It does something along the lines of the following
void ClearAllSlots(void) {
	for (int i = 0; i &lt; 63; i++) {
		ReleaseSlotByIndex(i); 
	}
}
Obviously, I didn’t know about ReleaseSlotByIndex at the time, but I put it in for clarity.
Anyway, I found out about a cool way that it keeps track of managed memory! Introducing the AllocedMemDescriptor. It looks something like this:
struct AllocedMemDescriptor {
	void * pData;
	int AssetType;
	int something;
	int something_else;
}
See the updated struct though, this is true as of the time I’m writing this…
Anyway, it has 2 tables full of these, and they store info on what memory the game has allocated, and what heap it’s from. assetType, best I can tell, says if it’s 3 or lower, it’s in “HEAP_BUNKI”. Otherwise, it’s in “HEAP_GAME”. The logic looks sorta like this:
FreeASsetDataWithHeapContext(AllocedMemDescriptor *param1) {
	if (param1-&gt;AssetType - 2U &lt; 2) // so is it 0-3?
	{
		MEM_PushNewHeap(HEAP_BUNKI)
	} else {
		MEM_PushNewHeap(HEAP_GAME)
	}
	DWfree(param1-&lt;pData)
	MEM_PopHeap();
	return;
}
You may notice the PushNewHeap and PopHeap. I’ll try to talk about those in the future, probably in some engine description thingy or something, idk. But put shortly, there are a whole buncha custom written allocator code, that does a buncha clever things helpful for a memory constrained system like the PS2 (since you’re constantly moving things in and out, and you don’t want fragmentation everywhere). So you have multiple heaps. Well, anyway, this program has a “heap stack”. So all of the DWFree or DWAlloc instructions operate on the heap at the top of the stack. I think DW in these contexts references DemonWare, which was apparently a company that made a ton of libraries to make networking code a lot easier, and that included some custom datatypes, and maybe, I suppose, some nice memory allocation libraries.
Interestingly, that struct (or at least I think its that struct, technically it could be a shortened version of the struct that only has the first two lines: the pointer and the AssetType… but Idk I assume it’s the same) is also contained inside of other classes/structs (again, hard to tell with how much reversing is still left to got!) So that means that there’s an easy and built-in way for the engine to say that a class “owns” a piece of allocated memory. Cool.
Anyway, that’s all, ciao!
